{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"HARNESS VERIFICATION ON THIS HOST: CONCLUSIVELY NOT POSSIBLE. Three runs, and the finding is firm enough to act on rather than keep burning time.\n\nRUN 1 (plain upstream entrypoint): died at ~25 min. Cause: 'DeployerWatchdog: Deployable [http://localhost:2990/cargocpc/index.html] failed to finish deploying within the timeout period [600000]' -> 'Cannot start container [Tomcat8xInstalledLocalContainer]'. Jira genuinely booted (JIRA Build 8.17.1#817001-sha1:36e93c711dfb...) and reached 'Starting the JIRA Plugin System'; Cargo's 10-minute deploy ceiling killed it.\nRUN 2 (-DstartupTimeout / -Dcargo.timeout): died the SAME way, still 'timeout period [600000]'. Those two property names have NO effect on the AMPS/jira-maven-plugin run goal. My guess was wrong and I am recording it as wrong.\nRUN 3 (-Dproduct.start.timeout=2700000, found in Atlassian's own docs, not guessed): ran 55 MINUTES with NO DeployerWatchdog kill at all — so **-Dproduct.start.timeout IS the correct knob** and the 600s ceiling is genuinely overridable. But it never reached 'Starting the JIRA Plugin System' (grep count 0); it was still crawling in the plugin's own surefire phase at 55 min. Emulation, not configuration, is the wall.\n\nNET FOR WHOEVER IMPLEMENTS THIS:\n * USE -Dproduct.start.timeout in the vendored Dockerfile's ENTRYPOINT. This is now empirically established, and it must carry a comment citing run 1's failure, or a maintainer will drop it and reintroduce a silent ~25-minute death that reads like a network fault.\n * -DstartupTimeout and -Dcargo.timeout are NOT the property names. Recorded so nobody repeats my two wasted runs.\n * A NATIVE amd64 RUNNER IS A HARD REQUIREMENT, not a preference. On emulated arm64 the instance does not become usable within an hour even with the timeout lifted.\n * ACCEPTANCE EVIDENCE FOR THIS STORY MUST NAME THE ARCHITECTURE IT WAS PRODUCED ON. Mine would have been produced on arm64 and is therefore unavailable; that is a fact about this host, not a defect in the plan.\n\nENVIRONMENT LEFT CLEAN: removed all three probe containers and the Maven cache volume, and deleted my two throwaway images. Kept ONLY 'pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1' (879MB) — the canonical upstream-named build — so a later amd64 attempt does not re-download ~900 Maven artifacts. Delete it with 'docker rmi pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1' if the space is wanted.\n\nSTATUS: this story remains OPEN and UNCLAIMED. I am not claiming a ticket whose acceptance criteria (AC1: the readiness probe reports REST answering; AC2: a smoke test creates and reads back an issue) cannot be satisfied on the available hardware. Claiming it to author files I cannot verify would put unproven work behind a green-looking board."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785367040910980001,"uuid":"4a1f0571-aeb0-46d5-ae6f-054e32e8173f"}